    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Raised in isolation under her mother's 'Kensington System', Victoria came to the throne at eighteen in 1837 and relied first on Lord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melbourne">Melbourne</a> and then, after their marriage in 1840, on her husband Prince Albert.</li><li>She tried privately to sway policy and appointments, favouring Disraeli, loathing Gladstone and endorsing an expanding empire whose title of Empress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> she took in 1876, while the monarchy's formal power continued to pass to Parliament.</li><li>Her withdrawal after Albert's death in 1861 fed a republican movement, but by her Golden and Diamond Jubilees she was again widely popular, the matriarchal figure of a family monarchy and a world empire.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Victoria had chloroform for the birth of her son Leopold in 1853 and again for Beatrice's in 1857, despite clergy who called it against biblical teaching and doctors who thought it dangerous.</p></aside>
    </section>

