      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1819</span><span class="rf-text">Born at Kensington Palace, the only child of the Duke and Duchess of Kent</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1837</span><span class="rf-text">Becomes Queen of the United Kingdom on the death of her uncle William IV, less than a month after her 18th birthday</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1840</span><span class="rf-text">Marries her first cousin Prince Albert of Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coburg">Coburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotha">Gotha</a> in the Chapel Royal of St James's Palace</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1861</span><span class="rf-text">Albert dies of typhoid fever in December, and Victoria withdraws into mourning and seclusion</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1876</span><span class="rf-text">Takes the title Empress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> under the Royal Titles Act, proclaimed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-durbar">Delhi Durbar</a> the next year</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1887</span><span class="rf-text">Celebrates her Golden Jubilee with a banquet for 50 kings and princes and a service in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1897</span><span class="rf-text">Her Diamond Jubilee is held as a festival of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>, with troops from all over the empire</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1901</span><span class="rf-text">Dies at Osborne House and is buried beside Albert at Frogmore</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Victoria was born at Kensington Palace on 24 May 1819, the only child of Prince Edward, Duke of Kent, fourth son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iii">George III</a>, and of Princess Victoria of Saxe-Coburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saalfeld">Saalfeld</a>, a widowed German princess. Her birth followed the death in 1817 of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princess-charlotte-of-wales">Princess Charlotte</a>, the king's only legitimate grandchild, which had pushed his unmarried sons to marry. She was christened Alexandrina, after her godfather <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a>, and Victoria, after her mother. Her father died in January 1820 and her grandfather a week later, leaving her third in line behind her uncles Frederick and William. When Frederick died in 1827 and George IV in 1830, William IV became king and Victoria his heir presumptive.</p><p>She later called her childhood "rather melancholy". Her mother and her ambitious comptroller, Sir John Conroy, raised her under the "Kensington System", a set of rules that kept her from most of her father's family and was designed to leave her weak and dependent on them. She slept in her mother's room, studied French, German, Italian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> with tutors, and was taken on tours of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> that annoyed the king, who saw them as royal progresses. She resisted every attempt to make Conroy her private secretary. In 1836 her uncle Leopold, King of the Belgians, brought his nephew Prince Albert of Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coburg">Coburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotha">Gotha</a> to visit; she wrote that he was "extremely handsome", though at seventeen she was not ready to marry.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Family in the encyclopedia</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/encyclopedia/princess-alice-of-the-united-kingdom">Princess Alice of the United Kingdom<span>Child</span></a></p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Victoria turned eighteen on 24 May 1837, so a regency was avoided, and on 20 June William IV died and she became queen. She dropped the name Alexandrina at once. Because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salic-law">Salic law</a> barred women from the Hanoverian succession, the crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a> passed to her uncle Ernest Augustus, ending the union of 1714. The Whig prime minister Lord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melbourne">Melbourne</a> became a powerful influence on the inexperienced queen, who probably saw him as a father figure. She was crowned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a> on 28 June 1838, became the first sovereign to live at Buckingham Palace, and paid off her father's debts.</p><p>Her early popularity suffered in 1839. Believing rumours that her mother's lady-in-waiting Lady Flora Hastings was pregnant by Conroy, she was implicated in a press campaign when Lady Flora was found to be a virgin and, at her death that July, to have had a tumour on her liver; the queen was hissed in public as "Mrs. Melbourne". The same year, when Melbourne resigned and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-peel">Robert Peel</a> was asked to form a government, she refused to let him replace her Whig ladies of the bedchamber, and Peel declined to govern, returning Melbourne to office. She proposed to Albert on 15 October 1839 and married him on 10 February 1840. Albert replaced Melbourne as her chief adviser, and relations with her mother, whom she had kept at a distance, slowly improved through his mediation.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Victoria and Albert had nine children between 1840 and 1857, who married into royal and noble families across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. In politics the queen sought to influence ministers and appointments. She supported the repeal of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corn-laws">Corn Laws</a> in 1846, quarrelled with Lord Palmerston for sending dispatches without her knowledge until he was removed in 1851, and worked to improve relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, becoming in 1843 the first British monarch to visit a French king since 1520 and in 1855 the first reigning British monarch in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in over 400 years. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-rebellion-of-1857">Indian Rebellion of 1857</a> she condemned atrocities on both sides and had the proclamation transferring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> to the Crown promise religious freedom. She supported the Reform Act 1867, which doubled the electorate, though she was not in favour of votes for women.</p><p>She was charmed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-disraeli">Benjamin Disraeli</a>, who said royalty should be flattered "with a trowel", and endorsed his expansionist policy, which led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-zulu-war">Anglo-Zulu War</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-anglo-afghan-war">Second Anglo-Afghan War</a>. His Royal Titles Act made her Empress of India from 1 May 1876, proclaimed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-durbar">Delhi Durbar</a> of 1877. She saw the empire as civilising and benign, writing that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> must be "Prepared for attacks and wars, somewhere or other, CONTINUALLY". She founded orders and decorations including the Victoria Cross in 1856, and in 1887 and 1897 her Golden and Diamond Jubilees became celebrations of the empire.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Victoria faced hostility at several points of her reign. In Ireland, where the Great Famine from 1845 killed over a million people and drove another million to emigrate, she was labelled "The Famine Queen". She gave £2,000 to the British Relief Association in 1847, more than any other individual donor; the story that she gave only £5 was a myth of the late 19th century. Her long seclusion after 1861 diminished the monarchy's popularity: in 1864 a protester posted a notice that Buckingham Palace was to be let or sold, and in 1870, after the founding of the Third French Republic, a rally in Trafalgar Square demanded her removal. Her reliance on John Brown, a manservant from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, and later on her Indian secretary Abdul Karim, the Munshi, drew gossip and her household's anger; she dismissed the complaints about Karim as racial prejudice. She was also the target of several attempts on her life, by Edward <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> in 1840 and others up to Roderick Maclean in 1882.</p><p>She in turn opposed ministers. Gladstone spoke to her, she is thought to have complained, as though she were "a public meeting rather than a woman"; she called his government "the worst I have ever had", blamed him for General Gordon's death at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khartoum">Khartoum</a> and was gleeful when his Irish home rule bill was defeated. Between 1877 and 1878 she threatened five times to abdicate to push Disraeli against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, without effect. Her biographers conclude that she was emotional, obstinate, honest and straight-talking.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In her last decades Victoria lost several of her children. Her daughter Alice died of diphtheria on 14 December 1878, the anniversary of Albert's death, and her son Leopold, who had haemophilia, in 1884. A fall at Windsor in 1883 left her lame and troubled by rheumatism thereafter. Her eldest daughter was widowed in 1888 after a little over three months as German empress, and her grandson became Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii">Wilhelm II</a>, whose autocratic views ended her and Albert's hopes of a liberal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. On 23 September 1896 she passed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iii">George III</a> as the longest-reigning monarch in British history. At the Diamond Jubilee of 22 June 1897 she sat in her open carriage outside St Paul's Cathedral for the service of thanksgiving.</p><p>With the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> War unpopular in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, she visited Ireland in 1900 for the first time since 1861, partly to honour its regiments. Her son Alfred died that July. She spent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> 1900 at Osborne House, as she did throughout her widowhood, disabled by rheumatism and with cataracts clouding her sight, and by mid-January she was drowsy and confused. She died there on 22 January 1901, aged 81, with her son Albert Edward and Wilhelm II present, and Albert Edward succeeded as Edward VII. Following her own instructions she had a military and white funeral, and was buried beside Albert in the Royal Mausoleum at Frogmore.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Victoria's reign of 63 years, seven months and two days was the longest of any British monarch, and of any queen regnant in world history, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-ii">Elizabeth II</a> passed it in 2015. She was the last of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hanover">House of Hanover</a>. Through her nine children she became "the grandmother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>"; 34 grandchildren survived to adulthood, and the haemophilia carried by her descendants appeared in the royal houses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. Modern speculation that her real father was not the Duke of Kent has no documentary support, and a spontaneous mutation, more common in the children of older fathers, is thought more likely.</p><p>During her reign the modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> took shape, as reforms of the vote increased the power of the Commons at the expense of the Lords and the Crown; in 1867 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-bagehot">Walter Bagehot</a> wrote that the monarch kept only "the right to be consulted, the right to encourage, and the right to warn". Only after her diaries and letters were published did the extent of her political influence become widely known. Her emphasis on morality and family life, against the scandals of earlier members of the House of Hanover, made the "family monarchy" one the middle classes could identify with. Early biographies such as Lytton Strachey's of 1921 are now considered out of date; those of Elizabeth Longford, Cecil Woodham-Smith and Stanley Weintraub are still admired.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/god-defend-new-zealand">God Defend New Zealand<span>1876</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Victoria reigned over the United Kingdom from 1837 to 1901 and became Empress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1876, presiding over an era of industrial change and imperial expansion. Privately she tried to steer ministers; publicly she became a symbol of personal morality and, after a long and unpopular seclusion following Prince Albert's death, the matriarch of a family monarchy and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s royal houses.</em></p>

